From: performance lane
Subject: [PATCH] SendModal: send the tender's technical column on the fallback
 match, and only ask about unresolved lines

Applies to: a9c61b3 (farqconstraction, src/views/SendModal.tsx clean at that
revision). Independent of the taxonomy_candidates join patch, which touches the
API repository only.

WHAT THIS IS
------------
SendModal calls POST /api/construction/boq/match a second time before creating
the RFQ, after the upload has already matched the same booklet. Two defects in
that call, one correctness and one cost:

1. It omitted the tender's technical column although item.spec is right there.
   The upload sends it; this call did not. So the fallback answered a poorer
   question and could name a different material. Measured on a real 69-line
   tender, two lines diverged:

       line-48  "وحدات إنارة"                    upload indoor-lighting / fallback lighting
       line-51  "أنابيب الباردة والحارة المياه"  upload ppr-pipes      / fallback pvc-pipe

   PVC and PPR are different materials sold by different suppliers, so the
   second one sends the RFQ to a pool that cannot quote the item. Sending the
   field makes both calls ask the same question: re-measured, the fallback now
   returns the upload's answer on both lines, and disagrees with the upload on
   0 of 69 lines.

2. It asked about every ready line, including the ones the upload had already
   resolved. buildRfqLinesFromItems reads item.farqSpecId first and consults
   specIdForLine only where it is empty, so those answers were discarded on
   arrival. On the same tender that is 69 lines asked where 30 are consumed.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE
-----------------------
The RFQ lines. Verified by content, not by count, against the real API answers
for that tender: the 69 built lines are byte-identical (JSON.stringify equal)
under all three payloads — as it was, narrowed, and narrowed with the spec.
39 lines carry a spec id, 30 carry null, each keeps its own name_ar and
original_name.

Sending the spec changes the answer for 0 of the 30 lines the fallback actually
feeds. Both divergent lines were resolved by the upload, so narrowing already
stops the fallback being consulted for them; the spec removes the class of
disagreement rather than one instance of it.

COST
----
One fewer full-booklet match per send: 2.7-3.4s and 2.13MB become ~1.9s and
1.52MB on this tender (30 lines instead of 69), and nothing at all when the
upload resolved every line. Fixed request cost per upload is unchanged at 21 —
this narrows the request, it does not remove it. Removing it needs a one-line
marker in parseBoq.ts, deliberately deferred while that file's lane repairs the
parser.

Note for the countdown lane: the send step's match is now proportional to
unresolved lines rather than to booklet size, and is skipped entirely when there
are none, so timing estimates calibrated against the old behaviour will run long.

TEST
----
src/lib/sendModalMatchNarrowing.test.ts, 6 assertions over a fixture that is
real API output for that tender (upload answers, spec-less fallback, narrowed,
narrowed-with-spec). The last one reads SendModal.tsx and asserts the payload
still carries the field, because the other five mirror the payload rather than
importing it — the handler builds it inline — and therefore pass even with the
field dropped. Verified: removing `spec: item.spec` fails that one test and only
that one.

diff --git a/src/lib/sendModalMatchNarrowing.test.ts b/src/lib/sendModalMatchNarrowing.test.ts
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4b325b3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/lib/sendModalMatchNarrowing.test.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,922 @@
+/**
+ * SendModal asks Farq to match the tender a second time before it creates the
+ * RFQ. That call is a fallback: buildRfqLinesFromItems reads item.farqSpecId
+ * first and consults specIdForLine only where it is empty. Two things follow,
+ * and both are guarded here, because the lines built on this path are the lines
+ * suppliers actually receive.
+ *
+ *   1. The call is narrowed to the lines that still have no spec id.
+ *   2. It sends the tender's technical column, which it used to drop even though
+ *      the item carries it. Dropping it made the fallback answer a poorer
+ *      question than the upload did: on a hot/cold water line it returned
+ *      pvc-pipe where the upload returned ppr-pipes — a different material sold
+ *      by a different pool of suppliers.
+ *
+ * Every map below is what the real API returned for a real 69-line tender.
+ */
